This recursive version defines a state by the two ending positions. If the ending characters match, the current run extends from the diagonal state. If they differ, the run ending at this pair is zero. The helper returns the longest value found while visiting the table.

```java
class Solution {
    private int[][] memo;

    public int longestCommonSubstr(String str1, String str2) {
        int n = str1.length();
        int m = str2.length();
        memo = new int[n][m];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            java.util.Arrays.fill(memo[i], -1);
        }
        return find(str1, str2, n - 1, m - 1, 0);
    }

    private int find(String str1, String str2, int i, int j, int best) {
        if (i < 0 || j < 0) {
            return best;
        }

        if (memo[i][j] != -1) {
            return Math.max(best, memo[i][j]);
        }

        int endingLength = 0;
        if (str1.charAt(i) == str2.charAt(j)) {
            endingLength = 1 + getDiagonal(str1, str2, i - 1, j - 1);
        }

        memo[i][j] = endingLength;
        best = Math.max(best, endingLength);
        best = find(str1, str2, i - 1, j, best);
        best = find(str1, str2, i, j - 1, best);
        return best;
    }

    private int getDiagonal(String str1, String str2, int i, int j) {
        if (i < 0 || j < 0) {
            return 0;
        }
        if (memo[i][j] != -1) {
            return memo[i][j];
        }
        if (str1.charAt(i) != str2.charAt(j)) {
            memo[i][j] = 0;
        } else {
            memo[i][j] = 1 + getDiagonal(str1, str2, i - 1, j - 1);
        }
        return memo[i][j];
    }
}
```

The empty-string case returns zero before any character access. This uses recursion, so the call stack can become noticeable for long inputs.

Time complexity: `O(n * m)`.
Space complexity: `O(n * m)` for the memo table, plus recursion stack space.

Instead of building matching lengths directly, this checks whether a common substring of a chosen length exists. For a fixed length, all substrings from the shorter string go into a set. Then the longer string is scanned for a matching block.

If length `k` is possible, every smaller length is also possible, so binary search can find the largest valid length.

For `"banana"` and `"ananas"`, checking length `3` finds blocks such as `"ana"`; checking larger lengths continues until the best common block is found.

```javascript
class Solution {
    longestCommonSubstr(str1, str2) {
        if (str1.length > str2.length) {
            const temp = str1;
            str1 = str2;
            str2 = temp;
        }

        let low = 0;
        let high = str1.length;
        let answer = 0;

        const exists = (length) => {
            if (length === 0) {
                return true;
            }

            const blocks = new Set();
            for (let i = 0; i + length <= str1.length; i++) {
                blocks.add(str1.slice(i, i + length));
            }

            for (let i = 0; i + length <= str2.length; i++) {
                if (blocks.has(str2.slice(i, i + length))) {
                    return true;
                }
            }
            return false;
        };

        while (low <= high) {
            const middle = Math.floor((low + high) / 2);
            if (exists(middle)) {
                answer = middle;
                low = middle + 1;
            } else {
                high = middle - 1;
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

The strings are not modified. This method is useful as a different way to view the problem, although creating substring slices and sets adds more work than the standard DP for many inputs.

Time complexity: `O(n * m * log(min(n, m)))` in the simple slicing model.
Space complexity: `O(min(n, m)^2)` in the worst case for stored substring contents.

The important difference from a subsequence problem is the reset on a mismatch. A matching character can extend only the diagonal value, so the current common part stays continuous.

The largest value seen anywhere in the table is the result.

```csharp
using System;

class Solution
{
    public int longestCommonSubstr(string str1, string str2)
    {
        int rows = str1.Length;
        int columns = str2.Length;
        int[,] dp = new int[rows + 1, columns + 1];
        int best = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i <= rows; i++)
        {
            for (int j = 1; j <= columns; j++)
            {
                if (str1[i - 1] == str2[j - 1])
                {
                    dp[i, j] = dp[i - 1, j - 1] + 1;
                    best = Math.Max(best, dp[i, j]);
                }
            }
        }

        return best;
    }
}
```

When the characters do not match, the cell is already zero because the table starts with zeros. This also handles two strings with no common characters.

Time complexity: `O(n * m)`.
Space complexity: `O(n * m)`.

I used a table where `dp[i][j]` means the length of the matching substring that ends at `str1[i - 1]` and `str2[j - 1]`. If the characters match, the value comes from the diagonal cell plus one. If they differ, it must be zero because a substring has to stay continuous.

For example, with `str1 = "abc"` and `str2 = "xbc"`, the matches for `b` and `c` build up to `2`.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
    public: int longestCommonSubstr (string str1, string str2){
        int n = str1.size();
        int m = str2.size();
        vector<vector<int>> dp(n + 1, vector<int>(m + 1, 0));
        int answer = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {
                if (str1[i - 1] == str2[j - 1]) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
                    answer = max(answer, dp[i][j]);
                }
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Empty strings are covered by the extra zero row and column. The input strings are not changed.

Time complexity: `O(n * m)`.
Space complexity: `O(n * m)`.
